WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.078 --> 00:00:07.541
Amal looked up as Mr. Patel went to the front of the classroom.

2
00:00:07.542 --> 00:00:10.303
"Listen up, class," he said.

3
00:00:10.304 --> 00:00:14.525
"I wanted to announce that we're going to put on a Thanksgiving play.

4
00:00:14.526 --> 00:00:17.013
We'll start rehearsing this week."

5
00:00:17.014 --> 00:00:25.018
"A Thanksgiving play?" Keisha groaned. "That sounds babyish, Mr. Patel."

6
00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:28.728
"It's not babyish," Mr. Patel said.

7
00:00:28.729 --> 00:00:34.114
"It's an excellent way to remind ourselves to be thankful for our blessings."

8
00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:41.349
"Ooh! Ooh!" John waved his hand. "Can I play the turkey?"

9
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.667
"I want to be a cranberry!" Nathan said.

10
00:00:45.668 --> 00:00:54.578
Everyone laughed so Amal joined in, even though she had no idea what was funny.

11
00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:58.679
Amal's family had moved from Syria last spring.

12
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:05.015
She'd learned a lot about living in America, but many things were still confusing.

13
00:01:05.016 --> 00:01:11.125
Right now it was cranberries and turkeys that were baffling her.

14
00:01:11.126 --> 00:01:15.771
A few other kids started complaining about the Thanksgiving play.

15
00:01:15.772 --> 00:01:21.769
Mr. Patel held up his hand. "No more complaints.

16
00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.333
We're putting on a play, and you're all getting a part." Amal

17
00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:30.688
bit her lip. That meant she was getting a part too. ***

18
00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:36.189
The next day Mr. Patel had a stack of play scripts on his desk.

19
00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:41.372
He glanced at a list while he called out part assignments.

20
00:01:41.373 --> 00:01:46.687
"Nathan and Keisha, you're going to play Native Americans."

21
00:01:46.688 --> 00:01:53.742
"John, your wish is coming true—you get to be the turkey!"

22
00:01:53.743 --> 00:01:56.784
"Amal, you're a Pilgrim, and—"

23
00:01:56.785 --> 00:02:02.275
"A Pilgrim?" Amal repeated. "What's a Pilgrim?"

24
00:02:02.276 --> 00:02:06.122
"Oh, Amal." Mr. Patel looked embarrassed.

25
00:02:06.123 --> 00:02:14.210
"I'm so sorry. Of course you're confused—this is your first American Thanksgiving."

26
00:02:14.211 --> 00:02:18.227
"I'll explain how it started!" Nathan volunteered.

27
00:02:18.228 --> 00:02:24.943
He quickly explained that the Pilgrims were a group of people that left England in 1620.

28
00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:31.710
They sailed to America on the Mayflower , seeking religious freedom in the New

29
00:02:31.711 --> 00:02:39.174
World. After they resettled in America, they faced many difficulties such as harsh weather and

30
00:02:39.175 --> 00:02:42.103
illness. Lots of people

31
00:02:42.104 --> 00:02:48.616
died. But with the help of Native Americans, the Pilgrims learned how to adapt to their new

32
00:02:48.617 --> 00:02:55.329
land. The Native Americans taught the Pilgrims how to plant corn and where to fish and

33
00:02:55.330 --> 00:03:00.677
hunt. "The following year was their first harvest," Nathan went

34
00:03:00.678 --> 00:03:05.944
on. "The surviving Pilgrims held a huge feast with the Native

35
00:03:05.945 --> 00:03:11.646
Americans. They gave thanks for their freedom and other blessings they'd found in

36
00:03:11.647 --> 00:03:17.168
America." "So a Pilgrim was someone who traveled to a new place?" Amal

37
00:03:17.169 --> 00:03:21.907
asked. "Yes, someone seeking a better life," Mr. Patel

38
00:03:21.908 --> 00:03:25.975
said. "Why did your family leave Syria?" Keisha asked

39
00:03:25.976 --> 00:03:31.533
Amal. "Because of the war." Amal swallowed

40
00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:35.308
hard. "Every night there were terrible

41
00:03:35.309 --> 00:03:42.163
bombings. Families started fleeing our city, and one day, my father said we had to leave

42
00:03:42.164 --> 00:03:46.809
too. We walked for hours, and planes were still dropping

43
00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.799
bombs. We were terrified that one would hit

44
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.538
us!" "Where did you go?" Nathan

45
00:03:54.539 --> 00:03:59.057
asked. "We boarded a bus and traveled to a refugee camp in Jordan.

46
00:03:59.058 --> 00:04:04.142
We lived there for nine months until a mosque here offered to sponsor us.

47
00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:07.319
They said they could help us resettle in this town."

48
00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.373
"Wow," John said. "Did your whole family resettle here?"

49
00:04:12.374 --> 00:04:14.465
Amal shook her head.

50
00:04:14.466 --> 00:04:18.165
"My other relatives went to different refugee camps.

51
00:04:18.166 --> 00:04:20.941
We haven't seen anyone since we left."

52
00:04:20.942 --> 00:04:23.721
Everyone was quiet.

53
00:04:23.722 --> 00:04:28.859
"Resettling in America has been hard," Amal went on softly.

54
00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.563
"But many people are helping us.

55
00:04:31.564 --> 00:04:37.931
Someone found a job for my father, and volunteers are teaching English to my mother."

56
00:04:37.932 --> 00:04:42.902
"Well, I think you're going to be a fantastic Pilgrim!" Nathan declared.

57
00:04:42.903 --> 00:04:50.303
"Just like the Thanksgiving Pilgrims, you left your home to start a brand-new life in a foreign country."

58
00:04:50.304 --> 00:04:54.522
"That takes so much courage!" Keisha added.

59
00:04:54.523 --> 00:04:58.841
Amal smiled as her classmates started applauding for her.

60
00:04:58.842 --> 00:05:02.877
"Life in America is often difficult," she thought.

61
00:05:02.878 --> 00:05:06.277
"But each day it gets a little easier."

